{"name":"Extra Life","hex":"#6ab417","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"extra","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Beyond what is due, usual, expected, or necessary; extraneous; additional; supernumerary.","Extraordinarily good; superior."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["To an extraordinary degree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/extra"},{"term":"life","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state of organisms preceding their death, characterized by biological processes such as metabolism and reproduction and distinguishing them from inanimate objects; the state of being alive and living.","The status possessed by any of a number of entities, including animals, plants, fungi, bacteria, and sometimes viruses, which have the properties of replication and metabolism."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To replace components whose operational lifetime has expired."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/life"}],"entries":[{"title":"Extra Life (fundraiser)","blocks":[{"text":"Extra Life is a fundraising event, the proceeds of which go to branches of the Children's Miracle Network Hospitals. 100% of all donations go directly to the hospitals."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extra_Life_(fundraiser)","wikidata":"Q25203383"},{"title":"Extra Life (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Extra Life is an American experimental band from Brooklyn, New York. Extra Life is known for their unusual rhythms and time signatures and for frontman and composer Charlie Looker's unique singing style, which often uses melisma and is inspired by medieval and Renaissance music."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Extra-Life-Band-2024-05-18-Jessica-Hallock-DSF2272.jpg/330px-Extra-Life-Band-2024-05-18-Jessica-Hallock-DSF2272.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extra_Life_(band)","wikidata":"Q1385144"},{"title":"Life (video games)","blocks":[{"text":"In video games, a life is a play-turn that a player character has, defined as the period between start and end of play. Lives refer to a finite number of tries before the game ends with a game over. Sometimes the euphemisms chance, try, rest and continue are used, particularly in all-ages games, to avoid the morbid insinuation of losing one's \"life\". Generally, if the player loses all their health, they lose a life. Losing all lives usually grants the player character \"game over\", forcing them to either restart or stop playing."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Lives_and_Health_in_gaming.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Life_(video_games)","wikidata":"Q3557866"},{"title":"Life","blocks":[{"text":"Life is the capacity in matter, formed of one or more units called cells, for processes such as cell signalling, homeostasis, metabolism, cell growth, adaptation, response to stimuli, and reproduction. All life eventually reaches a state of death. Many philosophical definitions of living systems have been proposed, such as self-organising systems. Defining life is further complicated by viruses, which replicate only in host cells, and the possibility of extraterrestrial life, which could be very different from life on Earth. Life exists all over the Earth in air, water, and soil, with many ecosystems forming the biosphere. Some of these are harsh environments occupied only by extremophiles. The life in a particular ecosystem is called its biota."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Coral_reef..._South_end_of_my_area_%2814119221571%29.jpg/330px-Coral_reef..._South_end_of_my_area_%2814119221571%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Life","wikidata":"Q3"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/af/1-UP_mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/af/1-UP_mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"An 1-Up Mushroom from the Super Mario series","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1-UP_mushroom.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG/500px-Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG/1280px-Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG/500px-Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Animal corpses, like this African buffalo, are recycled by the ecosystem, providing energy and nutrients for living organisms.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMale_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Adenovirus_transmission_electron_micrograph_B82-0142_lores.jpg/500px-Adenovirus_transmission_electron_micrograph_B82-0142_lores.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Adenovirus_transmission_electron_micrograph_B82-0142_lores.jpg/500px-Adenovirus_transmission_electron_micrograph_B82-0142_lores.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Adenoviruses as seen under an electron microscope"